# GOV.UK Design React — component development

This library ports [GOV.UK Design System](https://design-system.service.gov.uk/)
components to React. Every new component MUST match the GOV.UK Frontend markup and
CSS classes so that the official `govuk-frontend` stylesheet styles it correctly.

## Anatomy of a component

Each component lives in its own folder under `src/components/<Name>/` and ships four
things:

- `<Name>.tsx` — the component (default export).
- `<Name>.test.tsx` — Vitest + `@testing-library/react` tests.
- `<Name>.stories.tsx` — Storybook stories with `autodocs`.
- `types.ts` — only when prop/model types are non-trivial (see `Checkboxes`).

### Component file

- Use a **default export** function component: `export default function Name(props: Props) { ... }`.
- Start the file with a comment linking the GOV.UK page, e.g.
  `// https://design-system.service.gov.uk/components/warning-text/`.
- Type props as `type Props = { ... } & ComponentProps<'element'>` and spread the
  remaining native props (`{...props}`) onto the root element so consumers can pass
  `id`, `data-*`, `aria-*`, etc.
- Accept and merge `className`. Always merge with the `cx` helper from
  `@/utils/string.utils` — never hand-roll template-string class joining:
  `className={cx('govuk-warning-text', className)}`.
- Reuse the exact `govuk-*` class names and DOM structure from GOV.UK Frontend.
  When in doubt, mirror the HTML shown on the component's design-system page.
- Reuse existing building blocks instead of duplicating markup: `Label`, `Hint`,
  `ErrorMessage`, `FormGroup`, `Icon`, `Link`, etc.
- Compose `Link` (`src/components/Link/Link.tsx`) for any component that renders
  an anchor. Do not drop a raw `<a>` in new components (BackLink, SkipLink,
  Breadcrumbs items, PhaseBanner body links, and similar). When the GOV.UK class
  is not `govuk-link` (e.g. `govuk-back-link`, `govuk-skip-link`,
  `govuk-breadcrumbs__link`), pass `unstyled` so `Link` does not also apply
  `govuk-link`.
- Generate ids with React's `useId()` when a component needs to wire
  `aria-describedby`/`htmlFor` and the consumer did not supply an `id`.
- Prefer shared constants from `@/const` and `@/components/constants` (sizes,
  colours, layout, tag colours) over magic strings.

### Path aliases

Import via the `@/*` alias (maps to `src/*`), e.g. `@/utils/string.utils`,
`@/components/Hint/Hint`, `@/const`. Do not use long relative `../../` chains.

### Accessibility

Accessibility is a first-class requirement (the `@storybook/addon-a11y` addon runs
in Storybook). Use semantic HTML, correct roles, `visually-hidden` text where GOV.UK
does, and associate labels/hints/errors with inputs via `htmlFor`/`aria-describedby`.

## Tests (`<Name>.test.tsx`)

Cover at least: renders children/content, applies the correct root `govuk-*` class,
merges a custom `className`, forwards extra props (e.g. `data-testid`), and any
component-specific behaviour (variants, interactions, error/hint wiring). Query by
role/label/text (`@testing-library/react`) rather than test ids where possible.

## Stories (`<Name>.stories.tsx`)

- `title` follows the existing taxonomy: form controls use `Atoms/Form/<Name>`,
  other atoms use `Atoms/<Name>`, type/layout primitives use `Foundation/<Name>`.
  Add a new top-level group (e.g. `Molecules/`) only when nothing fits.
- Add `tags: ['autodocs']` and a `parameters.docs.description.component` that links
  the GOV.UK page. Include a `parameters.design` Figma link when available.
- Provide a `Default`/`Primary` story plus stories for the key variants and states.

## Wiring a component into the library

1. Export it (and any public types) from `lib/main.ts`, keeping the list alphabetised
   and matching the existing `export { default as Name } from '@/components/Name/Name'`
   / `export type { ... }` style.
2. Add a Changeset describing the change: create a file under `.changeset/` (e.g.
   `.changeset/<name>.md`) with `@rottitime/govuk-design-react` bumped `minor` for a
   new component (or `patch` for a fix). Example:

   ```md
   ---
   '@rottitime/govuk-design-react': minor
   ---

   Add `WarningText` component
   ```

## Before opening a PR

Run and pass, using Node 24 (`nvm use 24`):

- `npm run lint` — `tsc` + ESLint with `--max-warnings 0` (warnings fail).
- `npm run test:unit -- --run` — all Vitest unit tests.
- `npm run build` — the library build (`tsc && vite build`).

Prefer verifying the component visually in Storybook (`npm run storybook`) too.

## Pull requests per issue

- One component (issue) per branch and PR. Reference the issue in the PR body
  (e.g. `Closes #88`).
- Keep PRs focused; do not bundle unrelated components together.

## Responding to code-review feedback

When a reviewer leaves a comment on a PR:

1. **Apply it to the code.** Update the component/tests/stories to address the
   specific comment, then re-run lint, unit tests and build before pushing.
2. **Promote durable feedback into the rules.** If a comment expresses a
   *long-term, repeatable* preference (a convention, naming rule, a11y requirement,
   testing expectation, API-shape preference, etc.) rather than a one-off fix, update
   this rule file (or add a new rule under `.cursor/rules/`) in the same change so the
   convention is captured for every future component. Call out the rule update in the
   PR discussion so the reviewer can confirm it.
